\cf2 Have you noticed the way Ezekiel chapter 22 weaves together three different oracles? Before discussing what they do together, we ought to discuss their separate messages, starting with the first one. Each oracle communicates a coherent message which stands on its own, and we ought to look for it together. However, we can use the connections between oracles, as Ezekiel does, to elucidate the message of each oracle.\
The first oracle borrows some of its genre conventions from law courts, formally informing Jerusalem of the charges against it. But the phrase used to describe the object of this arraignment comes from Nahum's announcement of woe and the LORD's opposition towards the pagan city of Ninevah - the bloody city.\
But the accused here is Jerusalem, and the crimes, ones out of Leviticus 18 through 20. What had become commonplace, then and now, is brought into the conventions of a law court, with the language used to herald in advance the now accomplished punishment of Ninevah, Jerusalem's enemy, to shock the audience of exiles.\
Ezekiel has used this level of shock before, even the implicit comparison to pagan nations. But through the driving force of the appellation 'bloody city', Ezekiel is demanding our careful attention to his rhetoric, purpose, and methods.
