\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I do think the repetition in verse 2 is emphatic. But I think we have evidence that it ought not to be translated as a question, despite the grammar that would usually be translated that way. I think the grammar points to vehemence and certainty, rather than reluctance or impossibility that must be overcome. The same grammar functions that way in chapter 20 verse 4. Moreover, this reading of the grammar makes more sense of the shift to 'make known to her all her abominations'. But the exact valence of that vehemence, emphasis, or certainty would come from what follows.\
I read the infinitives of verse 3 as result. Idol making causes defilement is fairly clear, allowing us to read bloodshed as causing the coming of the city's time. This time is, in Ezekiel 7, alternately phrased as end, doom, and day. \
To answer your question, first we should note the way in which defilement and this time are parallel, as the bloodshed and idolatry are parallel, and together function as a summary or essence of a broad range of accusations which communicate a single idea - abomination, as in verse 2.\
Second, we have already noted the reference to Leviticus 18 in this oracle. Leviticus 18 warned the people that the abominations the people of Canaan practiced defiled the land, causing the LORD to punish the iniquity of the people, and the land to vomit out its inhabitants. This begins to explain how defilement and the time of judgement are parallel in verse 3.\
Third, the refining metaphor of the second oracle, and the comparison to the deluge as in Noah's day as a response to the corruption of the earth and the violence of the people, can somewhat help to interpret what is meant by these infinitives as result.\
Before I get to how you are reading verses 6, 9, and 12, I have a slightly higher level question. Within this legal format, are verses 6-12 additional crimes Jerusalem is accused of, or evidence presented for the crimes of the earlier verses? Both accusation and evidence are a way of, as in verse 2 instructs, making known the abominations. But evidence is at least lightly expected in this sort of legal judgement, as is the summons at the start, the punishment starting in verse 13, and the authority with which the oracle ends. Whether these verses are additional crimes or evidence at least moderately changes how we read them.
