Second, if 6-12 are charges, they have been separated from the charges in 3-4, though it would not be unheard of for Ezekiel to have a shorter charges and consequences, followed by a longer charges and consequences. However, if this is the case, the second set would not just be additional - they would be an expansion of and an explanation of the first.\
Another detail we need is the behold with which the section 6-12 starts. Not only does it start a new section, it focuses attention on the rulers.\
We should follow up your insight. Verses 6, 9, and 12 do create that frame, indicating that these violations are considered evidence of bloodshed. \
Though note, we have two charges being substantiated, only one of which is bloodshed. The other main charge, idolatry, may be what is discussed by cult meals on the mountains, for instance.\
But Ezekiel's audience, too, may have thought of bloodshed as limited to direct violence. And for that matter, they might have taken for granted many of these other violations, rather than seeing them as idolatry.\
For instance, making light of father and mother is still common today - but it is a very serious charge. First, the command to honor father and mother in Exodus 20:12 had been given a reason: that they might live long in the land the LORD their God was giving them. To keep it was to enter into God's promise. To violate it would then, as verse 4 puts it, bring their days nearer, that is the days of no longer being in the land. \
Second, the command to honor father and mother is classed not with the commands against people, as one might think, but against God himself. Fathers and mothers have their authority not from society, but from the LORD himself, and to dishonor them is not a social violation first, but an attack on God's authority as the sovereign God who put those parents there, and as the king over all authority in Israelite society - which fits with the way verses 6-12 discuss human authority in Jerusalem gone wrong. Recall the way verse 6 starts, focusing our attention on the leadership, and the way the subsequent verses follow up.\
But note also how making light of father and mother fits into the larger category of abomination which brings the LORD's wrath and causes the land to spit out its inhabitants. Like idolatry proper, making light of father and mother is a rejection of the covenant relationship under which the people are allowed in the land.\
Similarly, to oppress the sojourner, the fatherless, the widow is more than a social violation. Exodus 22 puts the explicit prohibition, in the same words, right after a ban on sacrificing to other gods, and it issues an explicit warning that the LORD himself will bring the sword on violators. Moreover, the LORD claims numerous times that such as the fatherless and the widow are under his special protection.\
But more seriously, these violations which appear to be without direct violence are an aspect of bloodshed committed by the strong against the weak. Verse 7 follows verse 6 emphasizing all the princes looking to their own arm, that is, their own strength. Whether this is using oppression to gain power, or using power to enable or legitimize oppression, one gets the sense of a society in which the strong do as they wish, and might makes right. What happens to the weak as in verse 7 is not just an example of might makes right, it is not just a social violation. What happens to the weak is a violation of the sanctity of all life. I use the word sanctity first, because of the holiness code references, second, because of the way these attacks on the weak are attacks on the LORD personally, and third, because of the larger discussion of shedding blood in the Bible, of which this is part. We often dismiss what is not literal murder, charged in law courts. But any casual disdain for human life, even not taking human life into account in matters which might not seem to call for taking into account human life (the example here being economic decision making), is bloodshed. And so I am very concerned at how verse 7 appears to you.\
Moreover, perhaps forgetting the LORD was left out because it did not seem to be part of the same list of violations. But, like the concept of abomination, forgetting the LORD can be used to sum up both bloodshed and idolatry. Considering the marriage metaphors earlier in Ezekiel, it is like a married woman forgot she was married; that would mean she considered herself no longer married, and had acted accordingly. There was no room in Jerusalem's heart for the LORD, so while if asked they would have said that the LORD exists and has the following commands the people ought to follow and covenant obligations towards the people, in practice they forgot everything but the feeling of security from the supposed covenant obligations. But as we have seen in the previous chapter, and from the parts of Exodus and Leviticus referenced in this chapter, those covenant obligations in fact obligate the LORD to punish his people under these circumstances. Also a thing the people seem to have forgotten.\
Presumably the other charges are more straightforward to integrate into this framework.\
Turning from the detailed textual evidence, we need to consider the rhetoric of the oracle. While the oracle is addressed to Jerusalem, and the subject is Jerusalem's guilt, the audience is presumably the exiles. Perhaps even more so than for other oracles, this oracle's themes ought to remind us that the exiles were the previous elite of Jerusalem, carried away under a previous king. They may have, in their time, been guilty of these same crimes in the same place. They may have hoped to go back to Jerusalem, and had they been permitted, they might have resumed much the same crimes. In any case, much of their hope was in Jerusalem's survival. How, in either the case of 6-12 being further crimes or heightened evidence, does verses 1-12 function as rhetoric with respect to the exiles? How well does the combined theory we are putting together fit all the evidence?
