\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Hand gestures like in verse 13 are difficult to interpret across centuries and cultures, so some uncertainty is appropriate. However, we have other uses of the gesture, as in Ezekiel 6:11, which may be somewhat clearer, by which to interpret it. There, this clapping of the hand is accompanied by a verbal expression which may mean much the same thing, say ah on account of all the evil abominations. That is, the clapping is an expression of righteous anger at evil and abomination. This fits - the grammar of verse 13 has the clapping as a response to evil and abomination as well. The bloodshed is objectively the sort of thing against which the wrath of God is revealed by this clapping.\
Verses 13 to 16 are to some extent an answer to the practical atheism of the people of verse 12, in which they forget the LORD. A God who acts will not be forgotten in that way - then they will know that he is the LORD, in a way they did not before he thus acted. That is, they ignored the LORD when he spoke, not understanding verse 14: I am the LORD, I have spoken, and I will do. The nature of these deeds of the LORD may have shocked the audience. But it ought not. Deuteronomy, in discussing what the LORD would do when his people rebelled against him, said openly that not only would the people be scattered among the nations, as in verse 15, but that they would continue to worship other gods there, and become a horror, a proverb, and a byword among the nations - so Deuteronomy 28:36-37. The people, even in exile, would continue to be unclean. Jerusalem, in verse 15, is rid of her uncleanliness by ridding it of her people.\
The passive grammar of defiling themselves then, points to this reality. The people would not give up the idols which defiled themselves, even in response to exile. Even if they were brought low in the eyes of the nations. Even if they were brought low in their own eyes, as Deuteronomy 28:66 promised that the LORD would do to them: "Night and day you shall be in dread and have no assurance of your life." Thus, the LORD asks in advance, in verse 14, can your courage endure? That is, when I deal with you, will you stand, or will your hope fail? If the hope of the people was a false hope, then perhaps we can understand why the LORD was so insistent on breaking that hope - that they might know that he is the LORD.\
This does seem to fit the disgrace from verses 4 and 5. The LORD's people are to recognize him as the LORD, and the disgrace and the resulting loss of false hopes will be causal in that. But the nations seeing the sin of the people may play a role in preventing misunderstandings by those nations about who the LORD is as well.\
Now that we have examined the individual components of the text in some detail, what is the rhetoric of the oracle as a whole, and what do we need to take into our discussion of the next oracle?
