\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Instead of my unsubstantiated opinion, I offer as a check on our interpretation whether the connections between the first and subsequent oracles are illuminated by our interpretation, or lead to anomalies that indicate we are missing something.\
Going forward into the second oracle, I would note that verse 16 suggests that the people are the source of the defilement, which fits with earlier verses.\
Both the first and the second oracle have a focus on what is within the city - note how often the second oracle uses a word meaning 'within' or 'midst'. And the transition between oracles mimics that of Isaiah 1:21-22, in which the city of murder (later expanded on, as Ezekiel expanded on bloodshed) is treated as a city full of dross (also expanded on). That transition may have been in view for the editor of the book of Ezekiel in putting these oracles together as much as the logical connections.\
Both Isaiah 1 and Jeremiah 6 might be referenced by the second oracle itself, though it might also be the case that ore refining was a common enough metaphor that no such reference is implied. In any case, that metaphor more or less is this second oracle. But that does not entirely answer the question of what meaning Ezekiel uses this metaphor in service to. To begin answering that question, I call attention to the discussion of gathering in the oracle.\
Logically, while the word gathering is opposite scattering, this instance of gathering would have happened before the scattering, and may even have led to it. That is, people would naturally flee to the fortifications of the capital city, Jerusalem, in the face of an advancing army. But when those fortifications fell, the people would have been gathered into one convenient location for slaughter or exile.\
But that may not be all that the gathering theme is doing in the rhetoric.
