The silver production occurs - the metaphor requires it - but the oracle's rhetorical focus is on 
\i them
\i0  experiencing the process. They are the dross experiencing the heat. They will know YHWH not because they become silver but because His fury proves undeniable in its intensity and effect.\
This prepares for the third oracle (vv. 23-31) by establishing that recognition comes through judgment, not before it. The first oracle showed what they were (bloody city full of abominations). The second oracle shows how they'll be forced to acknowledge YHWH's assessment through experiencing His refining wrath.\
What carries forward from both oracles into the third?
