\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Note, though that the LORD seeks a man to build a wall and stand in the breach. The LORD seeks a man to stand before him. As Ezekiel 18 notes, the LORD has no pleasure in the death of the wicked. He would prefer to find a man who should build up the wall and stand in the breech, a man who would call the people to repentance, that they might live.\
But this is not wishful seeking which pretends to find what does not exist - there was no such man.\
The prophets ought to have been such, but they instead were a conspiracy, a prey tearing lion, devourers of human life. As in Ezekiel 13, they did not go up into the breaches, but they smeared whitewash. The wall had a breech. They papered it over, rather than standing in the breech. They said what the people wanted to hear, what the people would have found helpful, rather than what was true. But think through the metaphor - there is a besieging army coming, the wall has a hole in it, and the prophets do what seems helpful - paper over the wall, so that the wall looks whole. But this is devastatingly immoral, because they lie to do it, and because the whitewash will immediately let through the invading army. Far from helping, they prevented others from taking warning.\
The priests ought to have been such a man building up the wall and standing in the breech, but they instead did violence to the law, and profaned what is holy to the LORD, neither teaching nor remembering. Because they neither taught nor remembered, the LORD was profaned. It was their responsibility to teach, that the people might know the law. But their skill with the law was used to distort the law. Now, we must remember that the people likely did not ask the priests to give them true distinctions between holy and profane and warnings to keep Sabbath. From the prophets, we know that much of what was said around the priests gave them excuses to ignore the LORD, and every reason to fear that speaking the truth would not be comfortable. But again, consider the metaphor of the breach in the wall. Whatever people want to hear, if the people are disregarding the LORD's holiness and committing bloodshed which leads to the LORD's wrath, is there not a duty to warn them? Is it not gravely immoral to stand aside, rather than building up the wall? Is it not even worse to do violence to God's law? However helpful they thought they were being, did that not end in ruin?\
The princes ought to have been such a man building up the wall and standing in the breach, but they instead sought their own gain. The princes ought to have been shepherds, leading the people in the way of repentance that leads to life, but they instead were wolves, tearing the flock. But however much they optimized for their own gain, what did they get? The LORD returned their way upon their heads. Blood for blood.\
Anyone ought to have been such a man building up the wall and standing in the breach. But the LORD found none.
