\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The breach metaphor exposes the catastrophic gap between appearing to help and actually helping. Each group optimized for what seemed useful from their position while ensuring destruction:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 The prophets' whitewash:
\b0  They knew the wall was breached. Rather than sound the alarm so others could repair it or prepare, they made it 
\i look
\i0  whole. This isn't passive negligence - it's active deception that prevents appropriate response. When the army comes through, those who might have fled or fought are caught unprepared because the prophets prioritized comfort over truth. The "conspiracy" (
\f2 \uc0\u1511 \u1462 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1512 
\f1 ) in v. 25 suggests coordinated effort to maintain the lie.\

\b The priests' violence to Torah:
\b0  
\f2 \uc0\u1495 \u1464 \u1502 \u1456 \u1505 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1514 \u1493 \u1465 \u1512 \u1464 \u1514 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  - "they have done violence to my Torah" (v. 26). Violence isn't too strong - they weaponized their expertise to distort rather than clarify. By failing to teach distinctions between holy/common, clean/unclean, they made navigation of covenant obligations impossible. The result: 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1464 \u1488 \u1461 \u1495 \u1463 \u1500 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1514 \u1493 \u1465 \u1499 \u1464 \u1501 
\f1  - "I am profaned among them." Their skill with Torah became the means of profaning the One whose Torah it was. They likely faced pressure to be "reasonable," to not impose "burdens," to be "pastoral." But hiding God's boundaries doesn't serve people - it exposes them to defilement they can't see coming.\

\b The princes' predation:
\b0  The shift from shepherds to wolves (v. 27) - those with responsibility to protect instead tear prey 
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1502 \u1463 \u1506 \u1463 \u1503 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1510 \u1465 \u1506 \u1463 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1464 \u1468 \u1510 \u1463 \u1506 
\f1  - "for the sake of dishonest gain." They optimized for wealth and power, presumably justifying it as effective governance. But verse 31's verdict 
\f2 \uc0\u1491 \u1463 \u1468 \u1512 \u1456 \u1499 \u1464 \u1468 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1512 \u1465 \u1488 \u1513 \u1464 \u1473 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1504 \u1464 \u1514 \u1463 \u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 
\f1  - "their way upon their head I have put" - shows their optimization produced exactly what they sought to avoid: destruction.\

\b The people of the land:
\b0  They weren't passive victims but active participants - 
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1464 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1511 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1465 \u1513 \u1462 \u1473 \u1511 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1490 \u1464 \u1494 \u1456 \u1500 \u1493 \u1468 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1490 \u1464 \u1494 \u1461 \u1500 
\f1  - "practiced oppression and committed robbery" (v. 29). The doubled verbs emphasize habitual, systematic practice. They oppressed poor and needy, wronged the sojourner 
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1456 \u1468 \u1500 \u1465 \u1488 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1460 \u1513 \u1456 \u1473 \u1508 \u1464 \u1468 \u1496 
