\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 While the text does say 'each', that need not imply independent operation. All princes engaging in self interest backed by power leading to coordination against the weak could just as easily be in view. \
Moreover, i am not entirely sure that verse 8 is in view in the bloodshed of verse 6. In favor, verse 6 is somewhat of a claim elaborated in subsequent verses. Against, there is alongside bloodshed the idols of verse 4 for verse 8 to be speaking to. However, verses 7, 9, and 12 do have this at best reckless indifference to the sanctity of human life. This is only possible because they have forgotten the LORD, the same with the idolatry.\
I agree that the text does not present this as a defended principle but as predatory behavior. The wolf imagery in verse 27 recalls the long-running metaphor of leaders as shepherds; if the shepherd is a wolf, woe to the sheep. The Bible repeatedly uses both good shepherds and predatory shepherds metaphorically. A wolf only cares about his self interest in eating the sheep, and may even delight in tearing the prey. A wolf seeks to have power over the sheep, not for the sake of power, but to eat the sheep using that power.\
The weakness of the widow, or the poor man lent money at interest, or whoever else is the target, is the occasion then for the predation, not the justification. And to the extent such indifference to the sanctity of human life is normalized in society, it needs no justification.
