\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You're right. The weakness is the 
\i occasion
\i0  not the justification. The wolf doesn't justify eating sheep - it sees vulnerable prey and takes it. When this becomes normalized, it requires no defense. It's just how things are done.\
This is actually more severe than a philosophical principle someone might defend. "Might makes right" suggests conscious justification that could be argued against. But normalized predation - where those with power simply take from the vulnerable because they can, and everyone treats this as unremarkable - operates beneath the level of justification. It doesn't need defending because it's not seen as problematic.\
The shepherd/wolf inversion (v. 27) makes this devastating. Shepherds have responsibility to protect. Wolves consume for their own benefit. When leaders become wolves - when those with power treat their position as opportunity to prey on those they should protect - the metaphor captures predatory self-interest replacing protective responsibility.\
The "eating" and "tearing" imagery throughout (vv. 25, 27) isn't abstract destruction. It's consuming lives for one's own sustenance - taking from others' vitality to increase one's own wealth, power, security. The widow's poverty, the debtor's desperation, the sojourner's vulnerability - these become occasions for those with strength to extract benefit.\
And verse 12's conclusion 
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1456 \u1488 \u1465 \u1514 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1464 \u1473 \u1499 \u1463 \u1495 \u1463 \u1514 \u1456 \u1468 
\f1  frames all of it. They didn't consciously decide YHWH's assessment didn't matter. They simply optimized for self-interest while forgetting that human life has sanctity before God. The forgetting made the predation possible - when God's claim on human life drops from consideration, power-holders naturally treat the vulnerable as resources to exploit.\
The text exposes normalized predation as bloodshed regardless of whether anyone defends it as principle.
