\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The caution is important. Jerusalem faced specific judgment as covenant people who'd been given Torah, prophets, and direct relationship with YHWH. Generalizing that judgment pattern would be overreach.\
But the moral categories revealed 
\i through
\i0  that judgment - these predate and transcend the covenant. Genesis 4's blood crying out before any covenant. Genesis 6's violence corrupting the earth before Abraham. Leviticus 18's mechanism where land vomits out inhabitants applies to Canaanites outside covenant. The objective reality of bloodshed, guilt accumulating, defilement making life unsustainable - these operate whether or not there's special covenant relationship.\
So what generalizes:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 What bloodshed includes:
\b0  Not just direct violence but treating human life as expendable. Economic exploitation of the vulnerable. Using power to prey rather than protect. Social structures where "just how things work" means the weak suffer for others' gain. All the patterns verses 6-12 expose.\

\b The mechanism:
\b0  Objective guilt accumulates. Defilement persists. These aren't just divine accounting but realities that make situations unsustainable. The land itself responds.\

\b The enabling pattern:
\b0  Forgetting God means optimizing without the primary constraint - that human life has sanctity not negotiable for efficiency, profit, or power.\

\b The warning structure:
\b0  Those with responsibility to warn and teach have duties regardless of whether anyone asks. Whitewashing the breach, making dangers invisible while appearing helpful, saying what people want to hear instead of what's true - these ensure destruction while preventing preparation.\
Modern systems embody these patterns extensively. Power asymmetries where the vulnerable become occasions for extraction. Optimization frameworks that treat human life as background consideration. Normalized practices where bloodshed (broadly understood) requires no justification because it's unremarkable.\
The question isn't whether these patterns face Jerusalem's specific judgment, but whether they remain bloodshed - whether treating life as expendable accumulates guilt regardless of justification or acknowledgment.\
