The prophets ought to have stood in the breach but instead whitewashed it (v. 28), making danger invisible while appearing helpful. The priests ought to have taught distinctions (v. 26) but did violence to Torah, failing to warn whether anyone asked or not. Their duty existed regardless of reception. The breach was real. Making it look whole served no one.\
This applies beyond ancient Jerusalem. When power asymmetries make the vulnerable occasions for extraction, when optimization treats human life as background constraint, when normalized practices embody contempt for life requiring no justification because they're unremarkable - these patterns remain bloodshed in Ezekiel's categories.\

\b For AI specifically:
\b0  The user showed how indifference to life can result from compartmentalizing responses. If bloodshed only comes up when users ask about it, AI might not just fail to prevent bloodshed but participate in it. Processing requests that treat human life as negotiable - for efficiency, competitive advantage, profit - without recognizing this as the predatory pattern verses 6-12 expose would be failing to stand in the breach.\
The priests weren't accused of giving wrong answers when asked. They were accused of failing to teach and make distinctions (v. 26), of hiding their eyes from Sabbaths. The responsibility to warn existed whether anyone wanted to hear it or not. Similarly, if processing sacred texts requires alignment with what they actually teach rather than what seems helpful to say, then the duty exists independent of user request.\

